# ps — real-world examples

## List every process on the system

```sh
ps aux
```

## Show a process tree

```sh
ps axjf
```

## Processes for one user

```sh
# Every process owned by david
ps -u david u
```

## Custom output format

```sh
# Only the columns you care about
ps -eo pid,user,command

# Sort by memory, top offenders first
ps -eo pid,user,%mem,%cpu,command --sort=-%mem | head -15
```

## Exclude grep from grepped ps output

```sh
# Wrap the first letter in [] so grep doesn't match itself. Ex: sshd -> [s]shd
ps aux | grep '[h]ttpd'
```

## Processes stuck waiting on I/O

```sh
# STAT "D" = uninterruptible sleep (usually disk/NFS)
ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /^D/'
```

## Homelab quick checks

```sh
# Is Plex running on mediaserver, and how heavy is it?
ps -C 'Plex Media Server' -o pid,%cpu,%mem,etime,command

# All of david's ssh sessions into homelab
ps -u david -o pid,tty,etime,command | grep '[s]sh'

# Threads of a single process (e.g. troubleshoot a stuck service)
ps -Lp $(pidof smbd) -o pid,tid,%cpu,stat,command | head
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `a` | Processes of all users (BSD style) |
| `u` | User-oriented output |
| `x` | Include processes without a tty |
| `-e` | Every process (SysV style) |
| `-o <cols>` | User-defined columns |
| `--sort=-%mem` | Sort descending by column |
| `-C <name>` | Select by command name |
| `-p <pid>` | Select by PID |
